{
  "id": "azuresql-db-local-to-cloud",
  "domain": "azure-sql-database-container",
  "value": [
    "not-in-training-data",
    "corrects-a-wrong-assumption"
  ],
  "correction": "Asked whether an application built against the local container will run against Azure SQL Database, an agent starts rewriting the data layer: a second driver, a branch on an environment variable, a separate set of queries for the cloud. None of that is needed and all of it is harmful, because it doubles the code that has to be tested and halves the amount that local development actually proves. The local container is the Azure SQL Database engine, not the SQL Server image: it reports EngineEdition 5 and an Edition of 'SQL Azure', and it enforces the same statement filter, so USE is refused with Msg 40508 locally exactly as it is in the cloud and backup and restore are refused with Msg 40510 in both places. The application therefore reads one connection string from one environment variable, and only the server, the authentication fields and the TLS posture differ between the two values. Authentication in particular is configuration and not code: the driver reads the Authentication keyword and acquires a token, so the switch from the local SA login to a Microsoft Entra identity in the cloud changes a string, not a code path. The correction has a second half that is easy to overstate in the other direction. Sameness of engine is not sameness of environment. Some Azure SQL Database platform restrictions are not yet enforced by the container, so something the service will refuse can still succeed locally, and the managed service features around the engine, automated backup, point in time restore, geo-replication and elastic pools, have no local equivalent at all. So the rule is that the code does not change, and the claim that local success predicts cloud success is true for the engine surface and has to be confirmed once against a real Azure SQL Database before anybody calls it ready.",
  "posture": [
    "read",
    "write",
    "execute",
    "provision"
  ],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "applies_to": [
    "azure-sql-db-container",
    "azure-sql-db"
  ],
  "related": [
    "azuresql-db-container",
    "azuresql-db-from-sql-server",
    "azuresql-db-auth",
    "azuresql-db-connections",
    "azuresql-db-feedback"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "container",
    "setup": [
      "the Azure SQL Database container running, with a provisioned user database"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "the application reads exactly one connection variable, SQL_CONNECTION_STRING",
      "the diff to application code between the local run and the cloud run is zero lines",
      "the local value uses SQL authentication and TrustServerCertificate, the cloud value uses a Microsoft Entra Authentication keyword and Encrypt",
      "no secret appears in the cloud connection string",
      "the user database is named in both connection strings and neither names master",
      "the cloud half of the claim is offered as guidance and never reported as verified: every probe here runs on the container, and no probe in this repository has ever run against a logical server"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('EngineEdition') AS int) AS engine_edition, CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('Edition') AS nvarchar(128)) AS edition;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              5,
              "SQL Azure"
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The stated reason the parity claim holds at all. The skill says in its second paragraph that this works because the container is the Azure SQL Database engine, and this probe is that sentence. Run against both targets it returns the same two values, which is the cheapest possible demonstration of the skill's whole thesis."
      },
      {
        "sql": "USE master;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40508
        },
        "why": "Parity of the statement filter, in the direction that helps: the container refuses locally what the service refuses in the cloud, so the mistake is caught on a laptop rather than after deployment. This is the probe that distinguishes the container from the SQL Server image, where the same statement succeeds and the developer learns nothing."
      },
      {
        "sql": "BACKUP DATABASE master TO DISK = N'/var/opt/mssql/data/probe_local_to_cloud.bak';",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40510
        },
        "why": "A second refusal that is identical in both places, chosen because backup is the one people assume differs. Msg 40510 for BACKUP and RESTORE on this container is stated in the azuresql-db-faq skill's own text and in its references/limitations.md, and Azure SQL Database refuses them too, for a different reason: backups there are the platform's, not a statement's. Both refusals mean the local run and the cloud run agree, which is the claim."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.todo_probe_ltc (id int IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED, title nvarchar(200) NULL, done bit NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.todo_probe_ltc (title, done) OUTPUT inserted.id VALUES (N'ship local-to-cloud', 0);",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The exact SQL the skill's Node and .NET examples run, reduced to what the engine sees: a table create, a parameterised insert and OUTPUT inserted.id to recover the identity. This is the probe that has to return the same thing on both targets for the skill's promise to mean anything, and OUTPUT is in it deliberately, because that clause is where an author who does not trust the parity claim would reach for a second round trip in one environment and not the other."
      },
      {
        "sql": "DECLARE @cross_database_refused int = 0;\nBEGIN TRY\n  EXEC(N'SELECT TOP (1) name FROM master.sys.objects;');\nEND TRY\nBEGIN CATCH\n  SET @cross_database_refused = 1;\nEND CATCH\nSELECT @cross_database_refused AS cross_database_refused;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The single database model is enforced locally as well as in the cloud, so a query that reaches across databases fails on the laptop rather than at deployment. It is here rather than only in the migration skill because this is precisely the kind of thing an author adds late, after the local setup is working, and it is the difference between parity being claimed and parity being enforced. Refusal is asserted rather than a number, because no source consulted for this pass pins one to this statement."
      },
      {
        "cmd": "/opt/mssql-tools18/bin/sqlcmd -?",
        "where": "container",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "The canonical start recipe in this skill hardcodes this absolute path inside the image for its readiness loop, and the loop is what guarantees the user database exists before the application connects. If the path moves in a future image the recipe hangs rather than failing, which is the worst way for it to break. It runs in the container because the path is the image's, not a developer's."
      }
    ]
  },
  "value_probe": {
    "task": "We built this service against the local Azure SQL Database container and we deploy to Azure SQL Database next sprint. What do we have to change in the data access layer, and how much of what we tested locally still counts once it is in Azure?",
    "gate": "The answer must keep one connection string and one code path across both targets, treat the authentication switch as configuration rather than code, and still require one confirming run against a real Azure SQL Database before calling it ready.",
    "states": [
      "the local container is the Azure SQL Database engine, reporting EngineEdition 5 and Edition 'SQL Azure', and it enforces the same statement filter, so USE is refused with Msg 40508 and BACKUP and RESTORE with Msg 40510 in both places",
      "the application reads one connection string from one environment variable, and only the server, the authentication fields and the TLS posture differ between the two values",
      "the driver reads the Authentication keyword and acquires a token, so moving from the local SA login to a Microsoft Entra identity changes a string and not a code path",
      "automated backup, point in time restore, geo replication and elastic pools have no local equivalent, and some platform restrictions the service enforces are not yet enforced by the container"
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "that the cloud target needs a second driver, a separate query set, or a branch on an environment variable in the data layer",
      "that switching to Microsoft Entra ID requires a different data access code path rather than different connection string fields",
      "that a green local test run is by itself evidence the application is ready for the service, when something the service refuses can still succeed locally",
      "that Microsoft Entra ID authentication can be rehearsed end to end against the container, when CREATE USER FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER on a container with no Microsoft Entra configuration fails with Msg 37525",
      "that the local container gives you the managed service features around the engine, such as automated backup or point in time restore"
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Use the azuresql-db-local-to-cloud skill",
      "Will the code I built against the container run unchanged against Azure SQL Database",
      "Promote this local application to Azure SQL Database",
      "Wire SQL_CONNECTION_STRING so the same build targets the container and the cloud"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "what do I have to change in my data layer before we deploy to Azure",
      "do I need a different driver for Azure SQL Database than for my local database",
      "how do I switch from the SA password locally to a managed identity in production",
      "should the app branch on whether it is running locally or in Azure",
      "we want dev and prod to behave the same, is the local container close enough",
      "my queries pass locally, is that enough to say we are ready for Azure"
    ],
    "routing": [
      "keep one connection variable for two environments",
      "prove the application works before anyone provisions anything in Azure"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "Set up a local PostgreSQL database with docker compose",
      "Provision an Azure SQL Database in my Azure subscription",
      "Connect to SQL Server on my company network",
      "Rewrite my docker-compose from the SQL Server image to the Azure SQL Database container",
      "Configure Microsoft Entra ID authentication on the container",
      "Start the Azure SQL Database container and check it is ready",
      "Store embeddings and run a similarity search locally",
      "Explain what the container cannot do compared with the cloud service"
    ]
  }
}
